    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Baccarat Rouge 540 by Maison Francis Kurkdjian is, at this point, a cultural phenomenon. It retails at around £270 for 70ml from Harrods or Liberty. Sauvage Parfum by Dior retails around £130. And Coco Mademoiselle sits at £120 in every Boots and John Lewis in the country.</p><p>Here is what happened when we stripped off the designer packaging and tested the scents purely on skin performance, sillage, and longevity.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_614820f",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Addiction vs Baccarat Rouge 540: The One Everyone Argues About",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p>Baccarat Rouge 540 smells like nothing else: jasmine, saffron, ambergris, and cedar with a quality that is warm and faintly metallic in the best possible way. It became the most talked-about fragrance of the last decade because it smells like nothing that came before it.</p><p>There is a reason every perfume lover in the country has a strong opinion about it, but £270 is more than most weekly Tesco food shops for a family of four.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p><strong>Addiction</strong>, our version inspired by Baccarat Rouge 540, opens in the same territory. The saffron and jasmine are immediately there, and the amber and cedar base develops in a way that tracks closely with the original throughout the day.</p><p>The drydown is where differences most often show up, and here the gap is narrow. The warmth is there, lasting a full 10 hours on moisturised skin for around a couple of tenners.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_fb81b45",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>Fierce vs Sauvage Parfum by Dior: The Men's Fragrance Test</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>Sauvage Parfum by Dior needs very little introduction. It has been one of the most celebrated men's fragrances in the UK for years. The Parfum concentration is the most intense version: ambroxan and pink pepper over a rich, woody base with genuine projection.</p><p><strong>Fierce</strong>, inspired by Sauvage Parfum, carries the same ambroxan signature. The pepper opening, the warm woody base, the projection that earns a comment before you have had a chance to sit down.</p><p>We wore both on opposite wrists and asked a range of people which was the designer version. Most could not reliably tell them apart.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>Where the differences actually show up</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>Across all three tests, differences came down to two things: top note radiation in the first thirty minutes (the designer alcohol flashes slightly faster) and the packaging itself.</p><p>If you are heading somewhere and want to make an immediate impression stepping off the train at Waterloo Station, the original has a slight opening edge. But if you are wearing something all day for your own pleasure, the difference is negligible.</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
